WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Luca: Good morning guys, I am with the legendary Lucrezia. Lucrezia: Hi everyone! Luca: we are on the wonderful

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:11.040
Gianicolo hill, and we'll talk about..what are we going to talk about Lucrezia? Lucrezia: Today we are going to talk a bit about

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.310
the difficulties that foreign students face when they learn Italian.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.180
Luca: Lucrezia is a fantastic Italian teacher, and has a Youtube channel

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.949
that has become a reference for all those who are learning Italian,

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:30.750
what is the name of the channel? Lucrezia: thanks, the name is "Learn Italian with Lucrezia" Luca: Fantastic, so, today

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.500
we are going to talk about - go figure, the Italian language, and we'll be doing so

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.760
by roaming around the beautiful streets of Gianicolo, because we currently find ourselves

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.399
in this wonderful neighborhood. Let's start with the first thing that you think is rather problematic for

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:49.110
Italian students. Lucrezia: So, the first that that comes to mind, offhand, is

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:56.100
prepositions, because prepositions follow certain rules in most cases but

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:02.640
there are quite a few exceptions and I find that

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.909
one should tackle this grammatical aspect of the language right from the get-go, and

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:15.720
one should continue to review them continually. I have come across students who

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.490
think they know how to use prepositions even when they speak Italian at a high level

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:27.090
but they don't feel like "going back to the drawing board" because

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.970
they think it is for beginners. Luca: Could you give us an example of one preposition that people normally

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:41.610
get wrong? Lucrezia: well, normally the propositions that people get wrong

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:51.030
are "di", "in", "a", as well as "per", and I want to dispel the myth that

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:56.009
prepositions are for beginners.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:00.689
Luca: Well, I agree with you. Lucrezia: I think prepositions are for every level,

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:08.129
for every student, and that's why one should review them all the time no matter their level. In this regard, I like using this metaphor

00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:12.510
of the learning process,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:18.269
that is, the concept of "spiral learning", according to which we move forward but then we go back to the previous points of the path, like in a spiral-like pattern

00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:22.620
Luca: I like the spiral-like learning curve, that's a nice concept!

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.599
Lucrezia: In this spiral-like method, students keep going back to some previous points from another point of view, from a higher and deeper perspective. Luca: fantasitc,

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:35.069
so, this is the first challenge. Lucrezia: I myself am talking too much, sorry about that! Luca: no no, that's totally fine!

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:43.200
Luca: what about the second challenge? A second challenge might be pronunciation and here,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:50.959
we can say a bunch of things, but I will limit myself to just y a few things. So, the sound

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:58.139
"ps", at the beginning of a word..Luca: "psicologico" (psychological) Lucrezia: yes, this sound is a bit problematic

00:02:58.139 --> 00:03:05.370
and then the sound "gn" and the sound "gli". Luca: "gli" is difficult even for some Italians,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.900
who often say "i" spaghetti" (instead of "gli"), and are not able to

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:17.970
pronounce "gli" correctly. Lucrezia: and then vowels. Sometimes students skip them,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:24.480
for example the "e" at the end of a word is often pronounced as "ii" (ee)

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.699
and most often than not, native English speakers tend to make this kind of mistake. Luca: that's true Lucrezia: and viceversa. Luca:

00:03:29.699 --> 00:03:32.609
another thing that comes to mind now is that Americans often say

00:03:32.609 --> 00:03:39.180
"gIovanni", they say "gIo", "cIo" and put say the "I"...Lucrezia: exactly. Luca: for example, they say "giulIani",

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:44.579
GIovanni..but in reality an Italian would say Giuliani. Lucrezia: that's right, so what do we learn from all this? That

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:50.340
prepositions..sorry Luca: no worries, keep going..Lucrezia: this makes us realized that in Italian

00:03:50.340 --> 00:04:00.230
vocals are always "full", maybe not always,, but 90 % of the time

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:10.620
because in the aforementioned cases the "i" (like in Giovanni) has the function of smoothening the sound. Luca: as when you

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.060
put sugar in your cup of coffee! Lucrezia: right, the function of the i is that of having us pronounce a soft "g",

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:27.349
does it make sense? Also..Luca: then..? Lucrezia: then another challenge is

00:04:27.349 --> 00:04:35.870
a la frase comparativa (sentence with comparatives), since in Italian we can choose between "di" and "che"

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:41.060
independently from what follows next, because it depends

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:47.080
on the grammatical function of the following word - whether it is an adverb, adjective etc, and so a lot of people

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:53.270
- filtering things through their native language - use only and always "che"

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:59.300
Luca: can you give us an example? Lucrezia: "Lui è più alto CHE stefano" (He is taller than Stefano)

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.940
and this sentence is incorrect. Luca: what should you say instead? "Lucrezia": lui è più alto DI Stefano", because Stefano is

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:11.840
a common name, so it requires "di" in front of it. Luca: wonderful. Guys,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:21.560
we have stopped here to make a pause because this is an interview "on the go" and it is a bit hard to walk and hold the camera so we are about to end the video..

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:29.060
Lucrezia: so, I'd say that another problematic point is la perifrasi (periphrasis) - starte + gerundio (gerund)

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:36.889
which in Italian indicates the so called "presente progressivo" - "quindi

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:42.409
adesso io sto parlando e Luca sta tenendo la videocamera" (so now I AM TALKING and Luca IS HOLDING the videocamera.. Luca: hardly!

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:47.900
Lucrezia: right, and you know why this is a problem?

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:54.949
Because students often use this structure as if they speaking their own language, and in the case of

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:59.800
native English speakers, this periphrasis is used to talk about the future

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.250
and this is obviously not correct, because in Italian we have a specific tense

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:10.969
to talk about actions unfolding in the future, so

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:16.599
in Italian we don't make any distinction between "futuro programmato" (planned future), "futuro incerto" (uncertain future) and

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:24.080
futuro spontaneo" (spontaneous future), and so on, we only have one tense for the future.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.870
Luca: And that's it! Lucrezia: and the one last point that I wanted to address is the

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:34.840
structure "tutto ciò che" (all/everything that), foreigners

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:41.260
have a hard understanding the role of "ciò" in this structure, and as a consequence I often hear

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:48.790
"tutto che", and an Italian can tell that "ciò" is missing

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.810
from this structure. "Tutto ciò che" is used together as a collocation

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.790
and the single roles of teach element gives meaning to

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:05.740
the overall structure. Luca: great, well said. Very informative! Do you want to add anything else? Lucrezia: well, no,

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:12.100
actually I am finished! Luca: (speaking in Roman) that's it let's end the video.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:16.330
Ok guys, thanks a bunch and thanks a ton to Lucrezia! Lucrezia,no thank YOU Luca

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:22.090
and I am sorry if the recording is not top-notch quality. Luca: well, this is actually the cool thing of this video

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:26.830
because if we had been sitting on some couch giving you a lecture you would be bored to death

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:32.560
Lucrezia: in the end, it is fun to see Luca and Lucrezia roaming around in the Gianicolo fields

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.640
Luca: yes, for grammatical fields, and in front of the famous Gianicolo Cannon

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.140
just a reminder that we are in Rome and from Rome

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:48.670
she comes from Roma Nord and I come from "Roma Sud", which is the same thing. Lucrezia: yes, although I have just moved up North!

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:54.130
Roma: yes, somewhere else in Rome. Guys, thanks a ton, have a great day and hugs! We'll see you soon

00:07:54.130 --> 00:08:01.100
here on YouTube. Ciao!

